  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender, Return Migration and Reintegration in the Gambia, Guinea and Senegal</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project&#x2019;s aim is to investigate, with an interdisciplinary perspective, how gender shapes return mobilities and the reintegration process of migrants, and how these impact upon gender in the context of Gambia, Guinea and Senegal. The focus is on three groups of returnees: migrants seeking institutional support before returning to Gambia, Guinea or Senegal; returnees only seeking support after arrival, and returnees seeking no support. This endeavour requires the joint effort of an interdisciplinary team: University of Fribourg, FHNW, University of Ziguinchor (SN), University of Conakry (GN), University of Kankan (GN), IOM (CH/SN) and GIZ (SN). The study is based on a multi-sited ethnography, using a variety of fieldwork methods. In order to investigate the temporal dynamics of return processes, the project will use a longitudinal study design. The project will engage with a theoretical framework consisting of three bodies of literature: gender studies, migration studies, and migration-development. The aim is to contribute to the theoretical discussion on linkages between gender and return. The expected project results enable a wide range of users: governments, IOs/NGOs to obtain knowledge on gender aspects inherent to return, so as to optimise return infrastructure.</narrative>
  </description>
